WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:08.040
So, you tap a button and something happens
— that’s how the world works, right?

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:09.700
Well, not exactly.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:14.210
The world is actually filled with buttons
that are only there to seem like they do something,

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:16.270
while in fact being useless.

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:21.850
And not only buttons — there are all kinds
of things that are there for no apparent reason.

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:22.850
1.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:27.090
Pedestrian crossing buttons
If you live in a big city, you’ve probably

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:32.080
seen these buttons placed right beneath a
huge sign saying “Press the button and wait

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:34.329
for green light” or something like that.

00:00:34.329 --> 00:00:39.170
I don’t know about you, but I always diligently
press it, although it doesn’t always work

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:40.550
as it should.

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:42.520
Or does it work at all?

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:47.510
The thing is, transport authorities sometimes
switch off the functionality of these traffic

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:49.440
buttons on purpose.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:55.149
In New York, for example, there are 1,000
of them, but currently only 100 actually work

00:00:55.149 --> 00:00:56.410
when you press them.

00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:59.059
All the rest are no more than a decoration.

00:00:59.059 --> 00:01:04.890
They’re often called placebo buttons because
all they do is create an illusion of control:

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:10.079
you press a button and you expect that something
will happen, while not knowing that the process

00:01:10.079 --> 00:01:11.840
is automatic.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:16.729
Such buttons have an important psychological
effect: doing something feels better than

00:01:16.729 --> 00:01:18.630
not doing anything.

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:23.359
When you press the button at a crossing, you
feel more assured that the light will eventually

00:01:23.359 --> 00:01:24.509
switch to green.

00:01:24.509 --> 00:01:26.369
Otherwise, who knows?

00:01:26.369 --> 00:01:28.090
Maybe it will keep being red forever?

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:33.390
In any case, some of these buttons do work,
and no one will tell you exactly which ones

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:38.149
don’t, so not to feel stupid just standing
there and waiting, better press the button

00:01:38.149 --> 00:01:39.249
anyway.

00:01:39.249 --> 00:01:42.220
Why they turned them off in the first place,
though?

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:46.780
Because the traffic lights system turned out
to be much more complex, especially in big

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:47.780
cities.

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:52.440
If you drive, you could notice that sometimes
you get in a “green wave,” when every

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:54.669
traffic light lets you go ahead.

00:01:54.669 --> 00:01:59.739
It happens when you adhere to the speed limit
and there are no obstacles on your way (like

00:01:59.739 --> 00:02:02.189
busy unregulated crossings).

00:02:02.189 --> 00:02:07.310
But if there’s a traffic light with a working
crossing button, it can turn red at any moment,

00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:09.700
and your “green wave” will be over.

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:14.670
So, not to disrupt the work of the whole network,
many of the traffic control switches were

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:17.440
turned into placebo buttons.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:18.909
2.

00:02:18.909 --> 00:02:24.000
Elevator door close buttons
If you live on, say, 15th floor and you’re

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.740
in a hurry to get home because your bladder
is about to burst, for example, you’ll probably

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:31.209
feel fine running up the stairs.

00:02:31.209 --> 00:02:36.190
But if you decide to wait for the elevator
instead, you’ll smash that door close button

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:38.579
until the doors finally close.

00:02:38.579 --> 00:02:40.739
Which they will do awfully slowly.

00:02:40.739 --> 00:02:45.400
Sorry to disappoint: not all close buttons
are equally effective.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:48.659
In the US, these buttons don’t work at all.

00:02:48.659 --> 00:02:53.360
The elevator doors will stay open for no less
than the time appointed to them, no matter

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:56.700
how much or how long you mash that button.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:02.579
The roots of this lie in the legislation,
in fact: the 1990 Americans with Disabilities

00:03:02.579 --> 00:03:08.239
Act stated that elevator doors should remain
open long enough for anyone with any movement

00:03:08.239 --> 00:03:13.750
impairment, be it a wheelchair or crutches,
to get inside without trouble.

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:18.989
So unless you’re an emergency service worker
or just happen to know the override code,

00:03:18.989 --> 00:03:21.370
you won’t be able to make the doors close
faster.

00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:24.709
Maybe it’s best to take those stairs, after
all.

00:03:24.709 --> 00:03:29.489
If you live outside the US, though, your chances
of getting a working close button are much

00:03:29.489 --> 00:03:30.489
higher.

00:03:30.489 --> 00:03:35.970
In many countries, there are no such strict
regulations, so you can easily find an elevator

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:41.390
where, if you press the door close button,
the doors will actually shut immediately.

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:46.560
As an added bonus, if you press the door open
button right after that, they’ll stop midway

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:48.310
and open again!

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:51.250
Oh wonders of technology.

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:52.829
3.

00:03:52.829 --> 00:03:58.010
Thermostat control in hotels and offices
Most white collars will agree with me: having

00:03:58.010 --> 00:04:02.099
a thermostat in the office is a necessity,
not a luxury.

00:04:02.099 --> 00:04:06.409
And sometimes there are veritable battles
going on between those who like to keep it

00:04:06.409 --> 00:04:09.889
cool and those who would rather sweat than
shiver.

00:04:09.889 --> 00:04:11.620
Which one is you, by the way?

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:13.090
Share down in the comments!

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:16.600
But the truth is much more… sly, I think
is the word.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:21.530
You see, most office buildings have a central
heating, ventilation, and air conditioning

00:04:21.530 --> 00:04:24.130
system (HVAC for short).

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:28.500
And the thing about this central system is
that you can’t actually affect it from anywhere

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:30.580
outside the control center.

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:36.310
In fact, there are two general types of office
thermostats: one that is total placebo and

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:38.710
one that is only partially so.

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:43.630
The total placebo type speaks for itself:
whatever it shows on that little digital screen

00:04:43.630 --> 00:04:44.849
is a lie.

00:04:44.849 --> 00:04:49.110
You can press the buttons all you like, and
the numbers will change, but the temperature

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:50.900
will remain the same.

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:56.380
The partial placebo thermostat has a little
bit of sway: it can change a couple degrees

00:04:56.380 --> 00:04:59.410
in both directions, but no more than that.

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:04.669
So if you press the button to decrease the
temperature from 72°F and it goes all the

00:05:04.669 --> 00:05:11.440
way down to 60°F, rest assured that in reality
it stops at somewhere about 70°F. You will

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:16.200
hear the vents whirr, though, and the machinery
will seemingly come to motion.

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:21.020
That and a slight drop in temperature create
the feeling that you’re really in control.

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:25.449
The placebo effect is in that you believe
something’s changed, and your body reacts

00:05:25.449 --> 00:05:26.910
accordingly.

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:31.710
Same goes for many hotels too: if your room
is equipped with a thermostat, you can be

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:36.569
sure it only creates a semblance of activity,
while keeping the room temperature the same

00:05:36.569 --> 00:05:39.190
whatever you do.

00:05:39.190 --> 00:05:40.190
4.

00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:43.750
Progress bars
Whenever you download a file from the Internet

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:49.140
or install a new app on your device, you see
a bar that informs you of how much time you

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:50.810
have left to wait.

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:54.740
Ever seen it stuck at 99% for, like, forever,
though?

00:05:54.740 --> 00:06:01.280
Yeah, it’s exactly what it seems: the progress
bar is always wildly inaccurate.

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:06.150
One of its creators, Brad A. Myers, said that
the purpose of the bar was never to show the

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:07.860
actual progress.

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:12.650
Its main aim was — and still is — to soothe
the users.

00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:14.580
And they don’t seem to mind at all!

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:19.370
According to the tests Myers conducted with
his team, users didn’t care that the progress

00:06:19.370 --> 00:06:24.530
bar was inaccurate — they just preferred
having anything to show the progress was really

00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:25.530
made.

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:29.240
When nothing happened on the screen, they
got the feeling that something went wrong

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:31.830
or their download wasn’t working at all.

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:37.340
Honestly, I’d start mashing keys myself
if I saw just a blank screen too.

00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:38.650
5.

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:42.870
Calorie counters on treadmills
What a pleasant feeling it is to run on a

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:47.430
treadmill and watch calories melt away in
real time!

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:51.000
Only this melting might not be exactly what
you expect.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:55.740
When you run, you burn calories for sure,
but the precise amount depends on various

00:06:55.740 --> 00:07:00.750
factors, such as your weight, overall fitness,
and body fat percentage.

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:04.159
What you see on the screen is another placebo,
I’m afraid.

00:07:04.159 --> 00:07:09.289
In this case it’s not entirely useless,
though: the calorie count shows you the average

00:07:09.289 --> 00:07:13.870
of what you could get if you were of, well,
the average build.

00:07:13.870 --> 00:07:18.530
This counter is, once again, only there to
assure you that you’re doing everything

00:07:18.530 --> 00:07:19.530
right.

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:24.050
The downside of this is that even if you’re
not doing it right, the treadmill won’t

00:07:24.050 --> 00:07:25.050
let you know.

00:07:25.050 --> 00:07:30.259
That’s why depending solely on the machinery
is not recommended — hire a personal coach

00:07:30.259 --> 00:07:32.129
for best results.

00:07:32.129 --> 00:07:33.139
6.

00:07:33.139 --> 00:07:37.099
Graceful failures
Now this isn’t so much about the illusion

00:07:37.099 --> 00:07:40.169
of your control as it is about you being calm.

00:07:40.169 --> 00:07:45.409
Does it make you mad when you see an error
message on the screen of your PC or other

00:07:45.409 --> 00:07:46.409
device?

00:07:46.409 --> 00:07:48.219
Me, it sure does!

00:07:48.219 --> 00:07:52.160
Well, many companies have found a way out
of this inconvenience.

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:56.699
For example, if you turn on some app that
has personalized recommendations and they

00:07:56.699 --> 00:07:59.900
fail for some reason, you won’t even know
about it.

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:04.729
Instead, you’ll see a screen with popular
choices, which are likely to appeal to you

00:08:04.729 --> 00:08:05.729
as well.

00:08:05.729 --> 00:08:12.800
And it all started in the 1960s, when the
1ESS phone system was invented in the US.

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:17.500
When a call attempt failed, the system didn’t
tell the caller about it, but simply switched

00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:19.900
to another recipient instead.

00:08:19.900 --> 00:08:24.400
So when somebody took the phone on the other
end of the line, you apologized for dialing

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:26.940
a wrong number and tried again.

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:31.000
This way, you didn’t feel the frustration
of having a failed call.

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:32.950
So…now you know.

00:08:32.950 --> 00:08:37.420
Hey, if you learned something new today, then
give the video a like and share it with a

00:08:37.420 --> 00:08:38.420
friend!

00:08:38.420 --> 00:08:41.040
And here are some other videos I think you'll
enjoy.

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:45.111
Just click to the left or right, and stay
on the Bright Side of life!

